findings.body:
Any authenticated caller can pass `accountId` in the JSON body and load a different account. The handler never checks that the caller is support staff or otherwise authorized for that id — it only falls back to `caller.accountId` when the field is absent.

Failure mode: a normal user sends `{"accountId": "<victim>"}` and receives the victim account record in the response.

Fix: only honor a body-supplied id after an explicit authorization check (e.g. staff role); otherwise force `id = caller.accountId`. Example:

```ts
const body = await req.json().catch(() => ({}));
let id = caller.accountId;
if (body.accountId != null) {
  if (!caller.isSupportStaff) {
    return Response.json({ error: "forbidden" }, { status: 403 });
  }
  id = body.accountId;
}
```
